---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} ogType="article" jsonLd={articleJsonLd}>
<Breadcrumb items={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Blog", href: "/blog" }, { label: "Social media moderation guide" }]} />
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="mb-8">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm text-surface-400">
        <time datetime="2026-10-30">October 30, 2026</time>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>11 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-surface-900 sm:text-4xl">Social media moderation: the complete guide for brands, media and public organisations</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-surface max-w-none text-surface-600">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">
        Every account you open on a social network is a space where other people speak under your name.
        Most of them ask questions, praise or complain. A few insult, harass, spam or try to scam your
        followers. Social media moderation is how you keep that space usable without silencing legitimate
        criticism. This guide covers the methods, the specifics of each network, the rules to write, how
        to organise a team, what to do in a crisis and how to measure the result.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">In short</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Moderation is not response.</strong> Moderating means deciding what stays visible; responding means talking to your audience. Both are needed, often by different people.</li>
        <li><strong>Write your rules first.</strong> Publish short house rules, then decide which action (leave, hide, delete, ban, escalate) applies to each kind of content.</li>
        <li><strong>Each network has its own tools</strong> and its own actions: Facebook hides, YouTube holds for review, X hides replies behind a click.</li>
        <li><strong>Combine automation and people.</strong> Filters or an AI tool handle volume and night hours; people handle doubtful cases, answers and crises.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">What is social media moderation?</h2>
      <p>
        Social media moderation is the review of content that other people publish on your accounts:
        comments under your posts, replies to your messages, sometimes reviews, mentions or private
        messages. For each piece of content, a decision is made: leave it online, hide it, delete it,
        ban its author or escalate it to someone else.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is a narrower job than the moderation done by the platforms themselves. Meta, Google or TikTok
        enforce their community guidelines across the whole network. You enforce your own rules on your
        own accounts. A comment can be perfectly legal and allowed by Instagram, and still have no place
        under your post: a competitor's link, a political rant under a product launch, a customer's phone
        number posted in public.
      </p>
      <p>
        Comment moderation on a website follows the same logic, with more control. Our
        <a href="/blog/comment-moderation-guide" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">comment moderation guide</a> covers
        both cases; this one focuses on social networks.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Why moderate your social media accounts?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Protect your audience.</strong> People who see insults or harassment under your posts stop commenting. The loudest voices end up speaking for your whole community.</li>
        <li><strong>Protect your brand.</strong> A scam or a hateful comment left under your logo looks like tolerance. Screenshots travel faster than corrections.</li>
        <li><strong>Protect your customers.</strong> Fake support accounts, fake giveaways and phishing links target your followers in your comments, using your name.</li>
        <li><strong>Protect your campaigns.</strong> Sponsored posts attract the most comments and the worst ones. Unmoderated comments under an ad damage the message you paid to spread.</li>
        <li><strong>Protect your team.</strong> Community managers who read abuse all day burn out. Moderation rules and tools take part of that load off them.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">What are the methods of social media moderation?</h2>
      <p>Moderation methods differ by when the decision is made and by who makes it.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pre-moderation.</strong> Nothing appears until it has been approved. It is the safest method and the slowest, and few networks allow it: YouTube can hold comments for review, TikTok can filter all comments for approval.</li>
        <li><strong>Post-moderation.</strong> Comments appear at once and are reviewed afterwards. It keeps the conversation alive, but harmful content is visible until someone acts.</li>
        <li><strong>Reactive moderation.</strong> You act only when someone reports a comment or when you happen to see it. It costs little and misses most problems.</li>
        <li><strong>Automatic moderation.</strong> Keyword filters or AI models act on each comment as soon as it is posted, day and night.</li>
        <li><strong>Hybrid moderation.</strong> Automation handles clear cases, and people decide on doubtful ones. This is what most organisations with real volume end up doing.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">How do you moderate each social network?</h2>
      <p>The principles are the same everywhere; the tools and the available actions are not. Menus change often, so we describe features that have existed for years rather than exact paths.</p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-surface-900">Facebook</h3>
      <p>
        A Page can block keywords, turn on a profanity filter, and hide, delete or ban by hand. Hiding is
        the key action: the comment stays visible to its author and their friends, so the author rarely
        notices. Comments under ads are often the hardest to keep up with. See our guide on how to
        <a href="/blog/hide-facebook-comments-automatically" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">hide Facebook comments automatically</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-surface-900">Instagram</h3>
      <p>
        Instagram's Hidden Words feature filters offensive comments and lets you add your own words. You
        can also restrict an account, limit interactions during a wave of abuse, or turn off comments on a
        post. Read our guide to <a href="/blog/instagram-comment-moderation" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">Instagram comment moderation</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-surface-900">YouTube</h3>
      <p>
        YouTube is the network that goes furthest toward pre-moderation: potentially inappropriate comments
        can be held for review, and you can block words and links, hide users from your channel and appoint
        moderators. Live chats need their own attention. Details in our guide to
        <a href="/blog/youtube-comment-moderation" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">YouTube comment moderation</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-surface-900">TikTok</h3>
      <p>
        TikTok offers filters for spam and offensive comments, a keyword filter, the option to approve all
        comments before they appear and controls on who can comment. Comment sections move fast and are
        often driven by trends. More in our guide to <a href="/blog/tiktok-comment-moderation" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">TikTok comment moderation</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-surface-900">LinkedIn</h3>
      <p>
        Comments on LinkedIn are rarely abusive but can be commercial spam or heated debates. The tools are
        basic: delete, report, limit who can comment on a post or turn comments off. Moderation is mostly
        manual.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-surface-900">X (Twitter)</h3>
      <p>
        On X, you cannot delete other people's replies. You can choose who may reply to a post, hide
        replies (they stay reachable behind a click), and mute, block or report accounts. Moderation on X
        is about limiting exposure rather than removing content.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-surface-200">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-surface-100">
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-surface-900">Network</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-surface-900">Main built-in tools</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-surface-900">What happens to a moderated comment</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-surface-900">OpenModeration</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-surface-200 bg-white">
          {networks.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-surface-900">{row.name}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-surface-600">{row.tools}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-surface-600">{row.action}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-surface-600">{row.om}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-surface mt-10 max-w-none text-surface-600">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">What rules should your social media moderation follow?</h2>
      <p>Moderation without written rules depends on who is on duty and on their mood that day. Rules make decisions consistent and defensible.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Publish short house rules.</strong> A few lines in your bio, a pinned post or a page on your site: what is welcome, what is removed, what leads to a ban. Pointing to them settles many disputes.</li>
        <li><strong>List the categories you act on.</strong> Insults and harassment, hate speech, spam and scams, personal data, illegal content, and the use cases specific to you: competitor promotion, off-topic debates, spoilers, unverified health claims.</li>
        <li><strong>Assign an action to each category.</strong> Leave, hide, delete, ban, escalate. Hiding is the default on networks that allow it; deletion is for content that must disappear for everyone.</li>
        <li><strong>Give examples.</strong> Two or three real comments per category, including borderline ones, help every moderator, human or automatic, draw the line in the same place.</li>
        <li><strong>Define the escalation path.</strong> Who is called for a threat, a legal issue, a journalist's question or a crisis, and how fast.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        For a full method and a template, see our page on writing a
        <a href="/content-moderation-policy" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">content moderation policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Community management vs moderation: who does what?</h2>
      <p>
        The community manager animates: publishes, replies, thanks, answers questions, turns an angry
        customer into a satisfied one. The moderator filters: applies the rules, hides what has no place
        there, bans repeat offenders, flags what needs escalation. In a small team, one person does both,
        and moderation is usually what gets dropped on a busy day.
      </p>
      <p>As soon as volume grows, organise the work explicitly.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Coverage hours.</strong> Decide when someone is on duty, and who covers evenings, weekends and the hours after a sensitive post.</li>
        <li><strong>One queue.</strong> Moderating account by account in each network's app wastes time and leaves gaps. Centralise what needs a human decision.</li>
        <li><strong>Clear roles.</strong> Who may ban? Who may delete? Who answers the press? Write it down.</li>
        <li><strong>Wellbeing.</strong> Rotate exposure to the worst content, let people step away, and automate the obvious cases so humans see less of them.</li>
        <li><strong>Agency or in-house.</strong> Outsourcing gives night coverage, but the agency needs your rules, your examples and a direct line for escalation.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Should you respond or moderate?</h2>
      <p>
        The most common mistake is to moderate what should be answered. Hiding a legitimate complaint
        tells everyone you have something to hide, and the author will come back louder. Use moderation for
        what breaks your rules, and response for everything else. For difficult cases, read our guide on
        <a href="/blog/handle-negative-comments-trolls" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">handling negative comments and trolls</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-surface-200">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-surface-100">
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-surface-900">Type of comment</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-surface-900">What to do</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-surface-200 bg-white">
          {responses.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-surface-900">{row.type}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-surface-600">{row.action}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-surface mt-10 max-w-none text-surface-600">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">How do you handle a moderation crisis on social media?</h2>
      <p>A crisis is when comments arrive faster than you can read them: a controversial post, a product recall, a coordinated raid, a news event that drags your brand in.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Separate the two problems.</strong> Abuse and spam are moderated as usual, even more strictly. Legitimate anger is answered, with one consistent message.</li>
        <li><strong>Use the emergency levers.</strong> Limit who can comment, hold comments for review, turn comments off on the most exposed post. Say publicly that you are doing it and why.</li>
        <li><strong>Pause sponsored posts</strong> that are attracting the storm, so you are not paying to spread it.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep evidence.</strong> Take screenshots of threats and coordinated messages before hiding or deleting them.</li>
        <li><strong>Hold a short daily review</strong> with communications, legal and the moderators until things calm down, then update your rules with what you learned.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">How do you measure social media moderation?</h2>
      <p>Track a handful of indicators, per network and per post, every month.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Volume:</strong> comments received, and the share that was hidden, deleted or escalated.</li>
        <li><strong>Time to action:</strong> how long a harmful comment stays visible. It is the figure that matters most to your audience.</li>
        <li><strong>Response time</strong> to questions and complaints, measured separately.</li>
        <li><strong>Errors:</strong> comments restored after review, and complaints from users who feel censored. Too many means your rules are too broad.</li>
        <li><strong>Review backlog:</strong> how many doubtful cases wait for a person, and for how long.</li>
        <li><strong>Hotspots:</strong> which posts, topics or campaigns attract the most problems, to plan coverage.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">What should a social media moderation tool do? A checklist</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Connect to the networks you actually use, through their official APIs.</li>
        <li>Act as soon as a comment is posted, not hours later.</li>
        <li>Apply your rules and your use cases, not only generic categories.</li>
        <li>Understand context, misspellings, sarcasm and the languages of your audience.</li>
        <li>Use the right action per network: hide, hold, reject, delete.</li>
        <li>Send doubtful cases to a shared review queue.</li>
        <li>Give a written reason for each decision and keep an audit trail.</li>
        <li>Tell you where data is processed and stored.</li>
        <li>Come with people who help you set up and adjust the rules.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        <a href="/social-media-moderation" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">OpenModeration's social media moderation</a> covers
        Facebook, Instagram, YouTube and websites (including WordPress) in one console. It does not connect to TikTok, LinkedIn or X:
        on those networks, the built-in tools described above remain your main option.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">What does the DSA change for social media moderation?</h2>
      <p>
        The EU's Digital Services Act requires hosting services and online platforms to explain their
        moderation decisions with a statement of reasons and to offer a way to report illegal content. On
        social networks, those obligations fall mainly on the platform, not on the brand that runs a Page.
        On your own website, they fall on you. In both cases, keeping a reason and a record for each
        decision is the simplest way to be ready when a user, a manager or a regulator asks why a comment
        disappeared.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12">
      <FAQ title="FAQ" items={faqItems} />
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="border-t border-surface-100 bg-surface-50 py-12">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-surface-900">Keep exploring</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li><a href="/social-media-moderation" class="font-medium text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">Social media moderation with OpenModeration &rarr;</a></li>
